  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nWindy conditions continue across much of western and central ND\nthis afternoon, with strongest winds north and central. Short\nterm guidance continues to indicate that most areas will see a\ngradual decrease in the strongest wind gusts after around 4 PM.\nThe Turtle Mountains area looks to have the best potential for\nstronger gusts 4 PM.\n\nA low pressure system over southern Manitoba is responsible for\nour strong winds this afternoon. A shortwave, which is currently\ntracking across the northern Rockies will move into the\nnortheast Montana this evening, then track across North Dakota\ntonight. This will bring showers and possibly a thunderstorm to\nmainly northwest and north central ND. There is another weaker\nwave that may bring a slight chance for showers to southwest \nand south central ND. Better chances tonight will definitely be\nin the northern half of the state. Precipitation amounts will be\nlight with most seeing under a tenth of an inch, with some areas\nseeing between a tenth and a quarter of an inch.\n\nWednesday will be another seasonably cool day with highs mainly\nin the 70s. It will be a little Wednesday but not to the extent\nthat a Wind Advisory will be needed, mainly westerly 15 to 25\nmph with some gust to 30-35 mph south central.\n\nAnother Canadian cyclone will bring a cold front through the\narea Thursday night. Ahead of the cold front, we are expected\nto see a brief but noticeable warm-up Thursday afternoon, with \nhighs climbing into the upper 70s to mid 80s over a good portion\nof western and central ND, with upper 80s forecast in west \ncentral and southwest ND. It may be breezy at times Thursday \nafternoon, but widespread windy conditions are not expected. It \nwill become breezy on Friday, behind the cold front, and we \ncould see an isolated shower as well. Temperature wise, Friday \nwill be a transition day with high temperatures ranging from the\nmid 60s in the northwest, well behind the front, to the mid 80s\nin the southern James River Valley, which may not see the front\nmove through until the afternoon. The cold front will usher in \ncooler temperatures that are forecast to remain over the area \nthrough the weekend. Forecast highs are expected to be in mostly\nin the 60s through the weekend, with some upper 50 degree \nreadings possible in the north. Overnight lows Saturday night \nmay drop into the mid 30s over some areas, especially in the \nnorth.\n\nAn approaching upper level low will bring more widespread\nprecipitation chances to the area late in the weekend and into\nnext week. A lot of uncertainty remains in the timing/placement\nof this system at this time it looks like we may see a good\nchance of widespread precipitation chances.",
